WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.535
We look at how to make business calls.

00:00:06.535 --> 00:00:11.055
Wilson and Wilson, can I help you?

00:00:11.055 --> 00:00:16.635
Yes, this is Lin Chan from Acme Appliances.
I'd like to speak to Mr Wilson if he's available

00:00:16.635 --> 00:00:17.635
please?

00:00:17.635 --> 00:00:20.905
Would that be Mr Wilson Senior or Mr Wilson
Junior?

00:00:20.905 --> 00:00:22.584
Mr Wilson senior.

00:00:22.584 --> 00:00:28.625
I'll just see if he's available - hold the
line please.

00:00:28.625 --> 00:00:33.765
It's a Lin Chan from Acme.

00:00:33.765 --> 00:00:38.605
I'm sorry, Mr Wilson's in a meeting at the
moment. May I take a message?

00:00:38.605 --> 00:00:46.635
Yes, could you ask him to phone me please?
My number's 23115654.

00:00:46.635 --> 00:00:48.157
I'm sorry, I didn't catch your name.

00:00:48.157 --> 00:00:51.245
Lin Chan, Acme Appliances.

00:00:51.245 --> 00:00:56.235
Let me check the number, 23115654.

00:00:56.235 --> 00:00:57.735
That's right.

00:00:57.735 --> 00:01:03.104
I'll pass that message on. Thank you.

00:01:03.104 --> 00:01:09.665
Thanks. Bye.

00:01:09.665 --> 00:01:12.275
Acme Appliances, Lin Chan speaking.

00:01:12.275 --> 00:01:14.625
This is Tom Wilson returning your call.

00:01:14.625 --> 00:01:20.635
Ah yes, Mr Wilson. Thanks for calling back.
I wanted to set up a meeting with you to discuss

00:01:20.635 --> 00:01:22.705
your requirements for next year.

00:01:22.705 --> 00:01:27.155
Yes certainly. How about Thursday about two-thirty.

00:01:27.155 --> 00:01:28.585
That would be fine.

00:01:28.585 --> 00:01:31.155
Okay, I look forward to seeing you then.

00:01:31.155 --> 00:01:34.394
Thursday, 2.30. See you then.
Goodbye.

00:01:34.394 --> 00:01:46.165
Goodbye .
When we use the phone we can't see the other

00:01:46.165 --> 00:01:52.415
person, so we have to listen carefully and
speak clearly. Often we deal with a switchboard

00:01:52.415 --> 00:01:59.064
operator or personal assistant, but the language
we use on the phone follows conventions.

00:01:59.064 --> 00:02:00.875
Wilson and Wilson, can I help you?

00:02:00.875 --> 00:02:06.975
Yes, this is Lin Chan from Acme Appliances.
I'd like to speak to Mr Wilson if he's available

00:02:06.975 --> 00:02:10.264
please?
When answering the phone, a switchboard operator

00:02:10.264 --> 00:02:15.345
will usually say the name of the company,
then "can I help you?" or "How can I help

00:02:15.345 --> 00:02:16.565
you?"

00:02:16.565 --> 00:02:21.965
Or they may not say anything after the name
of the company. In any case, the caller normally

00:02:21.965 --> 00:02:28.054
says their name, by saying 'this is' and their
name, then the name of their company after

00:02:28.054 --> 00:02:33.435
the words 'from' or 'of', and then who they
would like to speak to.

00:02:33.435 --> 00:02:39.985
Don't wait to be asked, but offer the information.
On the phone, unless you know the other party

00:02:39.985 --> 00:02:49.075
personally, always use polite, formal language.
Wilson and Wilson. What do you want?

00:02:49.075 --> 00:02:51.495
Lin Chan here. Put me through to Wilson.

00:02:51.495 --> 00:02:58.084
He doesn't want to speak to you.
Mr Wilson might not want to speak to Lin,

00:02:58.084 --> 00:03:04.745
but it's not polite to say this. Notice that
Lin says she wants to speak to Mr Wilson 'if

00:03:04.745 --> 00:03:06.825
he's available'.

00:03:06.825 --> 00:03:12.224
Often it's not convenient to speak to someone
straight away. 'If he's available' really

00:03:12.224 --> 00:03:17.194
means,
'If he wants to speak to me at the moment.'

00:03:17.194 --> 00:03:20.304
Here's some useful phrases for asking for
someone on the phone:

00:03:20.304 --> 00:03:27.625
Is Mr Wilson available please?

00:03:27.625 --> 00:03:33.534
Could I speak to Mr Wilson if he's available?

00:03:33.534 --> 00:03:38.045
Could you put me through to Mr Wilson?

00:03:38.045 --> 00:03:43.915
I'd like to speak to Mr Wilson if possible
please.

00:03:43.915 --> 00:03:49.245
So we can say:
I'd like to speak to Mr Wilson, or

00:03:49.245 --> 00:03:52.095
Could I speak to Mr Wilson? and

00:03:52.095 --> 00:03:55.834
If he's available , or
If possible

00:03:55.834 --> 00:03:59.465
And you always add please.

00:03:59.465 --> 00:04:03.745
And another phrase is:
Could you put me through please?

00:04:03.745 --> 00:04:08.205
The receptionist says:
"I'll just see if he's available," then, "hold

00:04:08.205 --> 00:04:10.814
the line please".

00:04:10.814 --> 00:04:16.055
But Mr Wilson isn't available, so this is
what she says:

00:04:16.055 --> 00:04:19.545
I'm sorry, Mr Wilson's in a meeting at the
moment.

00:04:19.545 --> 00:04:25.875
'In a meeting' is code for it's not convenient
for him to talk at the moment'. He may be

00:04:25.875 --> 00:04:31.915
in a meeting, but he could also be out, or
doing something else. Here's some phrases

00:04:31.915 --> 00:04:41.345
to practise, that can be used for this situation.
I'm sorry, he's in a meeting at the moment.

00:04:41.345 --> 00:04:46.775
I'm sorry, he's not available at present.

00:04:46.775 --> 00:04:55.205
I'm sorry, he's out of the office at the moment.
And here's one not to use.

00:04:55.205 --> 00:04:57.005
I'm sorry, he's busy.

00:04:57.005 --> 00:05:04.285
Too busy to talk to me obviously.
To say someone can't talk because they're

00:05:04.285 --> 00:05:11.055
busy, suggests that your call is not important.
But the receptionist knows what to say, and

00:05:11.055 --> 00:05:14.705
to ask if there's a message.
May I take a message?

00:05:14.705 --> 00:05:23.345
Yes, could you ask him to phone me please.
My number's 23115654.

00:05:23.345 --> 00:05:27.045
It's best to keep messages simple and to the
point.

00:05:27.045 --> 00:05:33.475
Here are a few simple phrases to use when
leaving a message. Practise them with Lin.

00:05:33.475 --> 00:05:38.335
Could you ask him to phone me please.

00:05:38.335 --> 00:05:42.684
Could you get him to return my call please.

00:05:42.684 --> 00:05:50.445
If he could call me back, that would be great.
Of course the important detail here is the

00:05:50.445 --> 00:05:55.975
actual phone number. It's important to pronounce
each number carefully.

00:05:55.975 --> 00:06:01.714
Two three, double one, five six five four.

00:06:01.714 --> 00:06:09.665
In America they would probably say:
Two three one one, five six five four. Try

00:06:09.665 --> 00:06:11.415
saying these numbers:

00:06:11.415 --> 00:06:25.765
oh four one four, six eight three one

00:06:25.765 --> 00:06:30.425
nine double eight two, six double seven six
or

00:06:30.425 --> 00:06:38.375
nine eight eight two, six seven seven six.
And the receptionist must also make sure she

00:06:38.375 --> 00:06:40.904
has all the details correct.

00:06:40.904 --> 00:06:45.404
Here are some phrases you can use to check
details.

00:06:45.404 --> 00:06:47.485
I'm sorry, I didn't catch your name.

00:06:47.485 --> 00:06:54.925
Could you just repeat the number please?

00:06:54.925 --> 00:06:59.375
Could I have your number again please?

00:06:59.375 --> 00:07:07.105
Would you mind repeating that?
When Tom Wilson returns her call, Lin answers

00:07:07.105 --> 00:07:11.475
like this:
Ah yes, Mr Wilson. Thanks for calling back.

00:07:11.475 --> 00:07:16.024
I wanted to set up a meeting with you to discuss
your requirements for next year.

00:07:16.024 --> 00:07:20.485
Yes certainly. How about Thursday at two-thirty?

00:07:20.485 --> 00:07:22.175
That would be fine.

00:07:22.175 --> 00:07:24.485
Okay, I look forward to seeing you then.

00:07:24.485 --> 00:07:26.705
Thursday, 2.30. See you then.

00:07:26.705 --> 00:07:27.714
Goodbye.

00:07:27.714 --> 00:07:32.825
Goodbye.
First Lin thanks him for calling back. She

00:07:32.825 --> 00:07:39.434
says, "Thanks for calling back". She could
also say, "Thank you for returning my call."

00:07:39.434 --> 00:07:45.554
Then she states the purpose of her call, and
they make the arrangements for the meeting.

00:07:45.554 --> 00:07:51.555
Because she wants the meeting, Lin lets Wilson
suggest a time. This is polite, because he

00:07:51.555 --> 00:07:58.105
is the customer in this situation. Then he
says, "'I look forward to seeing you then."

00:07:58.105 --> 00:08:03.934
Again, this is a polite way of ending a conversation,
as well as being a signal that there is no

00:08:03.934 --> 00:08:06.184
more to say.

00:08:06.184 --> 00:08:11.244
Notice too, that Lin repeats the day and time
of the meeting so that both people are sure

00:08:11.244 --> 00:08:13.505
about it.

00:08:13.505 --> 00:08:18.225
Let's now just review the key phrases for
phone calls when calling someone, and making

00:08:18.225 --> 00:08:19.895
an arrangement.

00:08:19.895 --> 00:08:28.715
Repeat them with the receptionist and Lin.
Wilson and Wilson, can I help you?

00:08:28.715 --> 00:08:30.115
I'll just see if he's available.

00:08:30.115 --> 00:08:34.095
Would you mind holding the line?

00:08:34.095 --> 00:08:41.215
Would you like to leave a message?

00:08:41.215 --> 00:08:46.625
Sorry, I didn't quite catch your name.

00:08:46.625 --> 00:08:51.355
I'd like to speak to Mr Wilson

00:08:51.355 --> 00:08:56.095
Could you put me through to Mr Wilson?

00:08:56.095 --> 00:09:01.145
This is Lin Chan returning your call.

00:09:01.145 --> 00:09:04.935
Thanks for returning my call.

00:09:04.935 --> 00:09:11.655
I look forward to seeing you then.
The key points when using the phone are to

00:09:11.655 --> 00:09:18.824
speak clearly and give essential information.
Don't speak too fast, and check that the other

00:09:18.824 --> 00:09:27.785
person has understood. If not, you may need
to rephrase. Use polite, formal language - these

00:09:27.785 --> 00:09:34.245
conventional phrases are signals for the other
person. We need to respond in the right way,

00:09:34.245 --> 00:09:40.285
or the conversation could be quite short.
Acme Applicances, Lin Chan speaking.

00:09:40.285 --> 00:09:42.264
This is Tom Wilson returning your call.

00:09:42.264 --> 00:09:46.165
Ah yes, Mr Wilson. Thank you for calling me
back.

00:09:46.165 --> 00:09:53.625
That's alright. Goodbye.
And it's goodbye from The Business of English

00:09:53.625 --> 00:09:55.715
for today. See you next time.